  Why choosing a σ that varies with x ?
  In practice, after discretization of the
  problem for numerical computation, if one considers a ﬁxed space step in the direction
  x inside the layer, the additional computational cost due to the PML layer is directly
  proportional to the width L : one thus have interest to reduce L as much as possible.
  If one refers to the expression (45) of the reﬂection coefﬁcient, this gives the feeling
  that it sufﬁces to choose σ > 0 constant and arbitrarily large to be able to take L
  arbitrarily small ! In practice, this is not true because one has to work with a numerical
  approximation of the wave equation. As a consequence, if the layer is effectively
  perfectly matched for the continuous problem, this is no longer the case for the discrete
  problem. This has been analyzed [11] when one considers the standard leap-frog
  scheme with centered second order ﬁnite differences. It is not my objective to come
  back here in detail the results of this analysis and I will simply describe qualitatively
