  By deﬁnition, the slowness curve of (78) is the curve of equation (81) in the slowness
  plane (kx/ω, ky/ω). On obtains in fact the union of two closed curves (that is why
  we shall speak of two slowness curves), symmetric with respect to the coordinate axes
  and with respect to the origin. In the isotropic case, these are two circles centered
  at the origin. Each line passing by the origin, parallel to K, crosses the slowness
  curves at four points (2 by 2 symmetric with respect to the origin) which are the four
  K/ω(|K|)’s. The two solutions of smaller modulus correspond to the rapid waves
  and are called quasi-longitudinal modes (these are the usual longitudinal modes in the
  isotropic case) and the two solutions of larger modulus correspond to the slow waves
  and are called quasi-shear modes (these are the usual shear modes in the isotropic case).
